src('SRC052','LOOK magazine, 1 July 1952: "Hunt For The Flying Saucer" (transcription)','J. Robert Moskin','LOOK / Project 1947 transcription','1952-07-01','Magazine article (transcribed)','SECONDARY','https://project1947.com/fig/look7152.htm','','whole article','%s'%A,'CLM098;EVD095;MIS041','"Lieutenant Ruppelt keeps 63 sightings on the top of his file ... the most detailed and most mystifying ... None of these 63 can be identified with any certainty ... pinpointed on a map ... a number of concentrations duplicated exactly the area of atomic energy installations." Also: reports flow from "our vital atomic installation sites"; Air Force had collected 800+ sightings.','Contemporary secondary; the author is a journalist, not Ruppelt; transcription of the original.','VERIFIED','EXAMINED')
src('SRC053','Early Top Secret UFO Document Discovered (USAFE TT #1524, 4 Nov 1948), Project 1947 - Jan Aldrich (MUFON Journal, Apr 2000, revised)','Jan Aldrich','Project 1947','2000-04 (revised)','Researcher article describing a declassified document','SECONDARY','https://project1947.com/fig/jtt.htm','TT #1524 Item #14','whole article','%s'%A,'CLM099;EVD096;MIS041','Item #14 covers Neubiberg sightings, Swedish intelligence viewing UFOs as possibly interplanetary, and an object crashing into a Swedish lake (witness a technical expert; Swedish recovery under way). Copies went to CIA and AMC. Aldrich links the lake event to Gen. Helge Jung’s 24 Aug 1948 aerial explosion seen as a Soviet-origin guided missile (per a separate TS memo).','Researcher description; original telecon not seen by me (War.gov copy blocked, 403).','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED','EXAMINED')
ev('EVD095',"LOOK 1 July 1952 (SRC052) vs the book's CLM098: the article is by J. Robert Moskin, not Ruppelt; the 63 sightings are Ruppelt's 'most detailed and most mystifying' unidentified cases in general (from pilots, ship observers, a colonel, scientists, weather observers and intelligence officers), not '63 cases of UFOs over nuclear installations.' The article says that when the 63 were mapped, 'a number of concentrations duplicated exactly the area of atomic energy installations', and that intelligence found no pattern/correlation otherwise and no evidence the saucers were spying on atomic plants. So the book's (Aldrich's) wording overstates the 63 as a nuclear-site file; the true statement is that clusters coincided with AEC areas and that the 63 file is not in declassified Blue Book files (the second part I did not test).",'TRANSCRIPT_COMPARISON','SRC052','CLM098;MIS041','13027','HIGH','CONTRADICTED')
ev('EVD096',"USAFE TT #1524 (SRC053) vs CLM099: Aldrich's description of the cable (4 Nov 1948, USAFE A-2 to Air Force DI, copies to CIA and AMC, Swedish lake object) is confirmed in outline by his own article, which I read; the Wikipedia-style 'existence of TS UFO documents at Wright Field' inference is his, and he himself says the Estimate of the Situation is separately unconfirmed. The cable's own content was mostly Neubiberg and Swedish-opinion items; the Swedish lake object is tied by Aldrich to a likely Soviet-origin missile sighting by Gen. Jung (24 Aug 1948), a context the book omits. The original telecon was not seen by me; a War.gov copy (2026 release) was blocked. The book's quoted Saturday Evening Post/Shallet restriction (Secret only, not TS) is from Aldrich's footnote and is not tested.",'TERTIARY_COMPARISON','SRC053','CLM099','13220','MEDIUM','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
